WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:02.180
- All right.
So, yes,

00:00:02.180 --> 00:00:05.040
we're here to talk about
the viewport matrix.

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:06.883
Why don't we start
with the words viewport?

00:00:06.883 --> 00:00:08.399
What does that mean?

00:00:08.399 --> 00:00:10.768
- The viewport is
the actual window

00:00:10.768 --> 00:00:13.061
that we're looking at
here at Cassidy's Cafe.

00:00:13.061 --> 00:00:14.848
The window you're looking at

00:00:14.848 --> 00:00:16.478
on a computer screen
is the viewport.

00:00:16.478 --> 00:00:18.767
The viewport is usually
just referred to

00:00:18.767 --> 00:00:21.429
or measured by

00:00:21.429 --> 00:00:24.730
the width and height of it,
but it doesn't have to be.

00:00:24.730 --> 00:00:26.785
So usually,
it would be zero, zero

00:00:26.785 --> 00:00:28.602
at the top left-hand corner,

00:00:28.602 --> 00:00:31.770
and then width and height
at the bottom right-hand corner.

00:00:31.770 --> 00:00:34.095
However, you can offset
 that a little bit,

00:00:34.095 --> 00:00:36.410
although I've never done it
and never tried it.

00:00:36.410 --> 00:00:38.607
But computer programming
 people are usually

00:00:38.607 --> 00:00:40.845
pretty good about
giving you options.

00:00:40.845 --> 00:00:42.525
- Yeah, you never know.
You might want to.

00:00:42.525 --> 00:00:44.205
- Yeah, so that just
makes the math harder.

00:00:44.205 --> 00:00:46.375
But if we assume that
it's just zero, zero

00:00:46.375 --> 00:00:48.538
at the upper left-hand corner,

00:00:48.538 --> 00:00:51.346
and then width and height at
the bottom right-hand corner,

00:00:51.346 --> 00:00:53.310
that's the viewport.
That's the window.

00:00:53.310 --> 00:00:54.827
- Viewport.

00:00:54.827 --> 00:00:56.968
- Yes, and all the stuff
that we're doing with models

00:00:56.968 --> 00:00:59.004
and model spaces,
camera spaces,

00:00:59.004 --> 00:01:01.200
all of these different things
all have to get

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:03.870
squeezed onto
this one screen.

00:01:03.870 --> 00:01:05.995
And so I need to find
the final process

00:01:05.995 --> 00:01:07.525
to do that.

00:01:07.525 --> 00:01:09.877
And that's this topic.
- Yes.

00:01:09.877 --> 00:01:11.845
So that's the first word,
viewport.

00:01:11.845 --> 00:01:13.525
It's kind of a weird word.

00:01:13.525 --> 00:01:15.825
Why isn't it just
called window?

00:01:15.825 --> 00:01:17.707
- Okay, let's change it.
- Okay, yeah.

00:01:17.707 --> 00:01:19.749
Okay, let's write someone
about that viewport.

00:01:19.749 --> 00:01:21.780
- That's the view...

00:01:21.780 --> 00:01:24.100
I'm viewing into this.
- A port.

00:01:24.100 --> 00:01:25.860
- Port.
- Oh, okay.

00:01:25.860 --> 00:01:27.460
Kind of makes me think
of being in the submarine

00:01:27.460 --> 00:01:29.063
looking out...
- The window.

00:01:29.063 --> 00:01:30.652
Isn't that what you're
looking at when you're looking

00:01:30.652 --> 00:01:32.183
at a little...

00:01:32.183 --> 00:01:34.772
- It all make sense now.
- Of course.

00:01:34.772 --> 00:01:37.575
- And it's the viewport matrix.

00:01:37.575 --> 00:01:40.698
So maybe I should explain
what a matrix is.

00:01:40.698 --> 00:01:43.536
Yeah, so I brought
myself a vector,

00:01:43.536 --> 00:01:45.381
X, Y, Z,
and I'm always

00:01:45.381 --> 00:01:48.360
keeping an eye on
this last component, the W.

00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:50.290
So what a matrix is,

00:01:50.290 --> 00:01:52.600
it's just a table of numbers.

00:01:52.600 --> 00:01:54.978
Like, this is four long,

00:01:54.978 --> 00:01:57.586
so the matrix has to have
a width of four,

00:01:57.586 --> 00:01:59.240
and a height of four.

00:01:59.240 --> 00:02:01.745
It's it's got to
match this somehow.

00:02:01.745 --> 00:02:04.630
It's got to have this
many columns or rows,

00:02:04.630 --> 00:02:06.219
depending on...

00:02:06.219 --> 00:02:08.119
We have
a column vector here,

00:02:08.119 --> 00:02:11.026
but some people like
 row vectors as well,

00:02:11.026 --> 00:02:13.105
where the vector
is across.

00:02:13.105 --> 00:02:15.900
But, yeah, we like
column vectors here.

00:02:15.900 --> 00:02:18.519
Yeah, so the viewport matrix,

00:02:18.519 --> 00:02:20.500
it's a four-by-four,

00:02:20.500 --> 00:02:23.116
so four columns, four rows

00:02:23.116 --> 00:02:25.100
to transform this thing.

00:02:25.100 --> 00:02:27.500
And this is a position.

00:02:27.500 --> 00:02:30.064
This is a position
in Cassidy's

00:02:30.064 --> 00:02:32.205
NDC box.

00:02:32.205 --> 00:02:34.127
So Cassidy made this for us.

00:02:34.127 --> 00:02:37.694
This is the normalized
device coordinate box.

00:02:37.694 --> 00:02:39.905
And the matrix takes us
from this box

00:02:39.905 --> 00:02:41.645
to the screen.

00:02:41.645 --> 00:02:44.133
And Cassidy's also set up
the camera in Unreal so

00:02:44.133 --> 00:02:47.459
that you have this huge
looking croissant at the front

00:02:47.459 --> 00:02:49.051
because it's closer to us.

00:02:49.051 --> 00:02:50.709
And at the back,
there's a sofa

00:02:50.709 --> 00:02:52.601
and a window, and they

00:02:52.601 --> 00:02:54.959
look much smaller
because of that perspective

00:02:54.959 --> 00:02:56.925
trick that you do to us.

00:02:56.925 --> 00:02:59.235
This NDC box, it has
a bunch of coordinates,

00:02:59.235 --> 00:03:01.545
X, Y, Z.

00:03:01.545 --> 00:03:03.667
X is left and right,
Y is up and down.

00:03:03.667 --> 00:03:05.305
The Z is always
a bit tricky one.

00:03:05.305 --> 00:03:08.242
The NDC box is left-handed,

00:03:08.242 --> 00:03:11.136
so X is positive to the right,

00:03:11.136 --> 00:03:14.440
Y is positive up,
but Z is going towards my nose.

00:03:14.440 --> 00:03:16.760
So the front
of this NDC box

00:03:16.760 --> 00:03:19.690
is negative Z.
But maybe we should...

00:03:19.690 --> 00:03:21.578
- Only in OpenGL.
- Sorry, carry on.

00:03:21.578 --> 00:03:23.533
Only in OpenGL,
and in Vulcan,

00:03:23.533 --> 00:03:26.833
it's now the right hand, okay?

00:03:26.833 --> 00:03:28.448
So just clarify.

00:03:28.448 --> 00:03:30.900
- It's almost like it's
a shared understanding

00:03:30.900 --> 00:03:33.501
of what space we're in,

00:03:33.501 --> 00:03:35.387
which way things point.

00:03:35.387 --> 00:03:37.225
- A lot of it is just
legacy as well.

00:03:37.225 --> 00:03:39.265
So the reason that
the viewport...

00:03:39.265 --> 00:03:42.150
As I was mentioning,
the upper left-hand corner

00:03:42.150 --> 00:03:45.060
is zero, the bottom right-hand
corner is width and height.

00:03:45.060 --> 00:03:48.839
And that comes from the old
CRT days, cathode ray tube,

00:03:48.839 --> 00:03:51.540
where the electron
gun is scanning along.

00:03:51.540 --> 00:03:53.235
And, of course,
you scan starting at

00:03:53.235 --> 00:03:55.400
the upper right-hand corner,
 and scan over

00:03:55.400 --> 00:03:57.364
towards the right,

00:03:57.364 --> 00:03:58.892
and then drop down
a little bit,

00:03:58.892 --> 00:04:00.645
scan again, scan again,
scan again.

00:04:00.645 --> 00:04:03.101
So TV sets,
old-style TV sets

00:04:03.101 --> 00:04:06.351
have always had
the zero, zero corner

00:04:06.351 --> 00:04:08.294
in the upper right-hand corner,
upper left-hand...

00:04:08.294 --> 00:04:09.794
- The left-hand corner, yes.

00:04:09.794 --> 00:04:11.433
- Yeah, and then
the width and height

00:04:11.433 --> 00:04:13.842
at the bottom right-hand corner.
- Yes.

00:04:13.842 --> 00:04:15.735
- So we just carried
on from there.

00:04:15.735 --> 00:04:17.242
- Yes, you're right.

00:04:17.242 --> 00:04:18.953
So maybe we should talk
about these coordinates.

00:04:18.953 --> 00:04:22.456
Yes, so on the screen
in pixel space,

00:04:22.456 --> 00:04:24.686
like Scott's saying,
the top left corner,

00:04:24.686 --> 00:04:27.734
that's zero, zero
for X and Y.

00:04:27.734 --> 00:04:30.514
But in our NDC box,

00:04:30.514 --> 00:04:32.834
the top-left corner,

00:04:32.834 --> 00:04:35.677
that's negative one for X,

00:04:35.677 --> 00:04:39.278
and positive
one for Y.

00:04:39.278 --> 00:04:41.342
So the matrix,
 this viewport matrix,

00:04:41.342 --> 00:04:45.139
is going to have to
transform that corner,

00:04:45.139 --> 00:04:47.631
that top-left corner
so it's equal to zero, zero

00:04:47.631 --> 00:04:49.475
on the screen.

00:04:49.475 --> 00:04:52.115
There's other parts
