automatically do that for me.

00:10:50.700 --> 00:10:53.580
But you wanted
to do ray casting.

00:10:53.580 --> 00:10:55.572
And so in ray casting,
 we're not actually going to

00:10:55.572 --> 00:10:57.660
project this onto a screen,
so now I have to

00:10:57.660 --> 00:10:59.225
do it the hard way manually.

00:10:59.225 --> 00:11:00.728
- Yeah, one of my assignments...

00:11:00.728 --> 00:11:02.265
actually, quite a few
of my assignments,

00:11:02.265 --> 00:11:04.745
you're clicking on the scene
with your mouse.

00:11:04.745 --> 00:11:06.649
You've got
the pixel coordinates,

00:11:06.649 --> 00:11:08.518
but I need to get
back into

00:11:08.518 --> 00:11:10.951
NDC space, and then
 eventually

00:11:10.951 --> 00:11:13.130
all the way to world space.

00:11:13.130 --> 00:11:15.045
So I use your viewport matrix,

00:11:15.045 --> 00:11:16.770
but I use it
going the other way.

00:11:16.770 --> 00:11:18.275
- So you take the inverse.

00:11:18.275 --> 00:11:20.090
- Oh, I love matrices.

00:11:20.090 --> 00:11:21.730
So, yeah, the matrices
take you from

00:11:21.730 --> 00:11:23.346
one space to the other,

00:11:23.346 --> 00:11:25.280
but if you want to go
the other way, like me,

00:11:25.280 --> 00:11:26.838
you just inverse it.

00:11:26.838 --> 00:11:29.356
- Of course.
- Wow. Okay.

00:11:29.356 --> 00:11:30.915
So we've talked
a lot about, like,

00:11:30.915 --> 00:11:33.523
the X and Y positions
of the front of

00:11:33.523 --> 00:11:36.845
this box and
where it is on the screen.

00:11:36.845 --> 00:11:38.600
But how about the Z?

00:11:38.600 --> 00:11:41.640
Do you do you need to mess
around with the Z coordinate

00:11:41.640 --> 00:11:44.528
as you transform
from NDC to pixel?

00:11:44.528 --> 00:11:48.128
- Yes, I need to record
those numbers, but what I'm...

00:11:48.128 --> 00:11:50.605
Now, this is going
to be trickier.

00:11:50.605 --> 00:11:52.911
So because these numbers go

00:11:52.911 --> 00:11:55.487
from positive Z

00:11:55.487 --> 00:11:58.276
towards negative Z,
but just one by one again,

00:11:58.276 --> 00:12:01.453
because it's a normalized
device, I can now scale

00:12:01.453 --> 00:12:04.560
those into real units,

00:12:04.560 --> 00:12:07.167
kind of, that match
what Cassidy's

00:12:07.167 --> 00:12:08.780
scene looks like.

00:12:08.780 --> 00:12:11.900
And I do that also
in the same matrix.

00:12:11.900 --> 00:12:14.396
So what I'm getting now is
I'm getting the Z depth,

00:12:14.396 --> 00:12:17.082
in the same units
 that the scene

00:12:17.082 --> 00:12:19.327
is drawn in,
but I'm storing that

00:12:19.327 --> 00:12:21.885
in a separate buffer
called the depth buffer.

00:12:21.885 --> 00:12:23.965
And I'm going to store
those numbers in there.

00:12:23.965 --> 00:12:25.925
And, again, that's just
so I can keep track of

00:12:25.925 --> 00:12:27.885
where things are
in the scene.

00:12:27.885 --> 00:12:29.885
Are they close to you?
Are they far away?

00:12:29.885 --> 00:12:32.707
And that just
becomes helpful.

00:12:32.707 --> 00:12:35.313
- I've heard about
this idea of, like, a...

00:12:35.313 --> 00:12:37.390
Kind of like a shakiness
in the pixels.

00:12:37.390 --> 00:12:39.870
I think Paul was mentioning
this, like Z fighting.

00:12:39.870 --> 00:12:42.024
- Z fighting.
- Oh, that's it, yes.

00:12:42.024 --> 00:12:43.606
- Yeah, so you're
getting complicated.

00:12:43.606 --> 00:12:45.134
This front surface here
is what we call

00:12:45.134 --> 00:12:46.713
the front clipping plane.

00:12:46.713 --> 00:12:49.389
Anything that is closer
to the viewer,

00:12:49.389 --> 00:12:51.675
from here,
it gets clipped out,

00:12:51.675 --> 00:12:53.275
so you don't see it.

00:12:53.275 --> 00:12:56.844
Anything that is
for the back,

00:12:56.844 --> 00:12:59.061
from the back
clipping plane,

00:12:59.061 --> 00:13:00.795
also gets taken
out of the scene.

00:13:00.795 --> 00:13:03.020
We can't see forever.

00:13:03.020 --> 00:13:06.247
Now, if you set up
the coordinate system

00:13:06.247 --> 00:13:09.061
in Cassidy's scene
such that the numbers...

00:13:09.061 --> 00:13:12.940
There's only a finite number
of numbers I can calculate.

00:13:12.940 --> 00:13:16.012
If you make that so big

00:13:16.012 --> 00:13:17.881
that there's really no
 difference between

00:13:17.881 --> 00:13:19.652
any two Z points,

00:13:19.652 --> 00:13:22.255
the depth of those points,

00:13:22.255 --> 00:13:25.955
then, well, sometimes
I think I'm in front of you.

00:13:25.955 --> 00:13:28.031
Sometimes the other pixel thinks
it's in front of another one

00:13:28.031 --> 00:13:29.700
and that's called Z fighting.

00:13:29.700 --> 00:13:31.227
And it just flickers,
and that just means

00:13:31.227 --> 00:13:32.760
you didn't set it up right.

00:13:32.760 --> 00:13:34.435
It's because
you didn't pay attention.

00:13:34.435 --> 00:13:36.822
- Another thought I just had
talking about

00:13:36.822 --> 00:13:40.083
