00:13:53.836 --> 00:13:56.324
But imagine, do you do
do you need the normals

00:13:56.324 --> 00:13:58.320
going from NDC
to pixel space?

00:13:58.320 --> 00:13:59.880
- That's all done.

00:13:59.880 --> 00:14:02.920
- Wow, so anything to do with
normals has already been...

00:14:02.920 --> 00:14:05.320
- The normals are just
mostly for lighting.

00:14:05.320 --> 00:14:07.800
- Right, so I can go back
to that one then.

00:14:07.800 --> 00:14:09.660
- Yeah.
- Yeah, just verticies.

00:14:09.660 --> 00:14:11.185
- Well, we're going to
divide that away.

00:14:11.185 --> 00:14:13.164
And then to get it into
two-dimensional space,

00:14:13.164 --> 00:14:14.665
I'm going to divide the Z away.

00:14:14.665 --> 00:14:16.178
- You're kidding.
That, too?

00:14:16.178 --> 00:14:18.176
- Yeah, that viewport

00:14:18.176 --> 00:14:19.912
is just in X and Y.

00:14:19.912 --> 00:14:21.425
- Yeah, just two numbers.

00:14:21.425 --> 00:14:23.725
Wow, we started with
four numbers, end with two.

00:14:23.725 --> 00:14:25.230
Okay.

00:14:25.230 --> 00:14:28.030
So I think we can summarize
what's been going on here.

00:14:28.030 --> 00:14:30.932
We're talking about
the viewport matrix,

00:14:30.932 --> 00:14:33.864
and that takes us
from the NDC cube,

00:14:33.864 --> 00:14:36.562
the normalized device
coordinate space,

00:14:36.562 --> 00:14:39.006
all the way
to pixel space.

00:14:39.006 --> 00:14:41.525
Our NDC cube,
it's pretty tiny.

00:14:41.525 --> 00:14:44.645
Yeah, it only has a width
of two, a height of two,

00:14:44.645 --> 00:14:46.484
a depth of two, and this is
quite different

00:14:46.484 --> 00:14:48.325
to the screen.

00:14:48.325 --> 00:14:50.327
This screen has a width of

00:14:50.327 --> 00:14:52.216
1,600 pixels

00:14:52.216 --> 00:14:55.080
and a height of
1,080 pixels.

00:14:55.080 --> 00:14:57.888
The origin of in NDC space

00:14:57.888 --> 00:14:59.720
is in the middle of the box.

00:14:59.720 --> 00:15:03.480
It's not true for pixel
space is the top-left corner.

00:15:03.480 --> 00:15:05.438
So, yeah, what your matrix
is really doing,

00:15:05.438 --> 00:15:06.995
and I like to
blame you for this,

00:15:06.995 --> 00:15:09.359
because I use it all the time
when I'm trying to figure out

00:15:09.359 --> 00:15:11.963
where my mouse is on the screen,
but get it into

00:15:11.963 --> 00:15:13.819
3D space.

00:15:13.819 --> 00:15:16.931
So I not only can use the
viewport matrix to go one way,

00:15:16.931 --> 00:15:18.791
you can inverse it to go

00:15:18.791 --> 00:15:21.383
from pixel to NDC space.

00:15:21.383 --> 00:15:23.340
- And all the way
